<Docs title="Architecture and credits" intro="How the shaders, browser library, and terminal renderer fit together.">
<h2>Web rendering</h2><p>The web package owns the GPU runtime, camera, shader pipeline, and glyph analysis. This site imports its React component; the explorer uses the same public API as other consumers.</p><div class="pipeline"><span>Scene + camera</span><b>→</b><span>Light + bloom</span><b>→</b><span>ASCII / raw</span></div><p>WebGL2 and WebGPU backends expose the same visual controls. Quality presets trade render resolution and sampling work against detail. ASCII is a rendering stage: it maps the scene's structure into glyphs, rather than placing a text texture over a photograph.</p>
<h2>Terminal rendering</h2><p>The Rust renderer produces frames for the Ratatui interface. The published Ghostty export is a separate background shader. A replacement using the canonical scene and ASCII pipeline is in development; see the <a href="/docs/terminal/#ghostty">Ghostty guide</a> for release status.</p>
<h2>Origins and credits</h2><p>Blackhole was extracted from <a href="https://github.com/austindelic/portfolio">Austin Delic's portfolio</a>. It keeps the renderer and its ASCII treatment in reusable packages.</p><ul><li><a href="https://departuremono.com/">Departure Mono</a> by Helena Zhang supplies the interface typeface, distributed under the SIL Open Font License.</li><li><a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> and <a href="https://react.dev/">React</a> power the static site and interactive island.</li><li><a href="https://ratatui.rs/">Ratatui</a> and <a href="https://ghostty.org/">Ghostty</a> provide the terminal surfaces.</li></ul><p>Asset provenance and license notices live in <a href="/ASSET_NOTICES.txt">the asset notices</a>. Refer to the <a href="https://github.com/austindelic/blackhole">source repository</a> for code licensing and shader attribution.</p>
<h2>Working on Blackhole</h2><p>Keep renderer changes in the package, interface changes in the explorer, and terminal adaptations in their integration directories. Verify the actual rendered result on the target platform; compilation alone does not verify appearance or performance.</p>
</Docs>
